  "body": {
    "id": "prop:classical-matroid-corollary",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "As a classical corollary of the graphic-matroid union theorem in \\cite{Edmonds1965}, \\(r_q(E)=\\min_{F\\subseteq E}\\{|E\\setminus F|+q(|V|-c(F))\\}\\). Applied after the attainable-row theorem and the tree-packing criterion of \\cite{NashWilliams1961}, its disjoint-forest and minimizing-set outputs certify whether the causal operator can generically attain rank \\(q(|V|-1)\\); no new combinatorial min--max theorem is claimed.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "r_q",
      "E",
      "q",
      "V",
      "G",
      "c_F"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:matroid-certificate",
      "thm:generic-tree-rank",
      "lem:edmonds-matroid-union-rank",
      "lem:nash-williams-tree-packing"
    ],
    "justification": "The cited classical certificate makes the new causal rank conclusion auditable without treating the certificate itself as a contribution.",
    "gap": "The min--max identity is classical; the only project-specific content is that Theorem 6 makes it a certificate for attainable proximal-network rank.",
    "consumer": "Evidence-network curators can attach a short classical certificate to the causal conclusion that a proposed graph does or does not exhaust its proxy-limited information.",
    "proof_tex": "The cited matroid-union rank theorem states that the rank of the union of \\(q\\) copies of a matroid with rank function \\(r_M\\), on ground set \\(E\\), is\n\\[\n\\min_{F\\subseteq E}\\{|E\\setminus F|+q\\,r_M(F)\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nFor the graphic matroid of the spanning multigraph \\((V,E)\\), a maximal forest in \\((V,F)\\) has one fewer edge than vertices in each component, and hence\n\\[\nr_M(F)=\\sum_{j=1}^{c(F)}(|V_j|-1)=|V|-c(F).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nThe independent sets of the union are precisely edge sets partitionable into \\(q\\) pairwise disjoint forests, so its rank on \\(E\\) is the defined quantity \\(r_q(E)\\).  Substituting (2) into (1) proves\n\\[\nr_q(E)=\\min_{F\\subseteq E}\n\\{|E\\setminus F|+q(|V|-c(F))\\}.\n\\]\nMoreover, \\(r_q(E)=q(|V|-1)\\) if and only if the maximizing forest family consists of \\(q\\) forests each having \\(|V|-1\\) edges, hence of \\(q\\) spanning trees.  This is also the tree-packing condition characterized by the cited Nash--Williams partition theorem.  The forest family certifies the lower bound, and a minimizing \\(F\\) in the display certifies the matching upper bound.  Combining this classical certificate with the generic-tree-rank theorem gives exactly the claimed causal-operator certificate; the min--max identity itself remains the cited classical result.",
